 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed; the certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The registry keeps a local embedded SQLite index on each kiosk. Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. No lookup path reaches the network; the index is rebuilt from the signed snapshot when a new snapshot arrives.
+
+## Index Structure
+
+The index is a single SQLite database file with two tables:
+
+- `snapshot`: one row with the snapshot date, schema version, and signature manifest fingerprint.
+- `records`: one row per equipment record with `serial`, `description`, `calibration_date`, `due_date`, and `serial_key`.
+
+`serial_key` is the normalized lookup key: surrounding whitespace trimmed and ASCII letters uppercased, with punctuation unchanged. A unique index on `serial_key` prevents duplicate lookup keys. `serial` preserves the original text for display and export.
+
+The index is rebuilt from the snapshot on every import. It does not merge with or incrementally update the active index.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using the bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate that serial numbers are unique after normalization. Duplicate raw serials and serials that differ only by surrounding whitespace or ASCII letter case are rejected.
+
+Build the new index in a temporary database file in the same directory as the active index. Insert every record and validate the completed index before activation. Activate the new index by atomically replacing the active database file. If any step fails, delete the temporary file and leave the previous active index in place; report a useful error. A lookup never observes a partially built index.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active index by `serial_key` and return the matching record or `NotFound`. Return the active snapshot date in every result. If the active index cannot be opened, report an error rather than returning `NotFound`.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index on each kiosk | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Keeps lookups on local files and avoids a service to run or network dependency; accepts per-kiosk index rebuild and storage when a snapshot arrives. |
+| Snapshot activation | Rebuild a separate index and atomically replace the active file | Update the active index in place; append-only records | Rebuild avoids partial or stale records; atomic replacement preserves the previous snapshot on import failure. |
+| Lookup key | Store a normalized `serial_key` and enforce uniqueness on it | Normalize only at query time; store raw serials only | A stored normalized key gives deterministic lookup and catches duplicates that normalize to the same value. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Index size and performance tuning for 90,000 records and 150 MB snapshots; validate against the 250 ms lookup goal during implementation.
+2. Recovery behavior if the active index is corrupted outside an import.
